=== What this measures, and why it did not exist before ===
SPIKE-FINDINGS.md states plainly: "No transcript from Plans 18-01/18-02
captured install/download wall-clock timing... This is recorded here as an
honest gap rather than a fabricated number." That gap covers download time,
not per-call scoring time, but the same honesty rule applies to
SCORING_TIMEOUT_MS (src/foundry/collaborative-scoring-bridge.ts): its
600000ms value has been a reasoned default since Plan 21-01 ("A reasoned
default, not a measured one... `runScoringPreflight`'s probe-first warm-up
call is what produces the FIRST real measurement (Plan 21-04), not a re-read
of this comment") because no raw/ transcript anywhere in this repo carries a
real per-call wall-clock number for score_one.py. THIS log is that first real
measurement: one live runScoringPreflight warm-up call, run against the real
provisioned venv, the real score_one.py, the real revision-pinned KB, and the
committed prime-pool-manifest.json / fingerprint-manifest.json fixtures as
the expected side.

=== The command run ===
A throwaway script (never committed — needs network, the 254MB KB load, and
minutes of wall clock in the general case, none of which belong in `npm
test`), invoked from the main repo root so the bridge's repo-relative path
constants resolve to the real venv/data:

  $ cd <repo-root> && npx tsx <scratch>/score-one-walltime-probe.ts

The script: reads the two committed manifest fixtures, parses them through
the bridge's own parsePoolManifest/parseFingerprintManifest, builds a
plausible 20-entry ranked prediction over real in-pool ids (1..20, bounds
0..129374), and calls runScoringPreflight with queryId=97 (read from
test/fixtures/stark/prime-selection.json's own meta/first-pair query_id
field only — no gold field was read or referenced) against the real venv
(tools/stark-eval/.venv/bin/python) and the real tools/stark-eval/score_one.py.

=== A pre-existing bug this run surfaced, and the workaround used to reach the timed call ===
observeFingerprint's version-probe (Plan 21-01, src/foundry/collaborative-
scoring-bridge.ts, UNCHANGED by this plan) issues one python -c call:
`import sys, torch, stark_qa; print(sys.version.split()[0]);
print(torch.__version__); print(stark_qa.__version__)`. Against the REAL
provisioned venv this call itself fails two independent ways, confirmed live
this session:
  (a) `import stark_qa` prints "WARNING: faiss must be imported for
      indexing" to STDOUT (a real stark_qa startup wart) ahead of the three
      expected print lines, shifting every subsequent line index by one.
  (b) `stark_qa.__version__` does not exist as an attribute at all —
      confirmed live: `AttributeError: module 'stark_qa' has no attribute
      '__version__'`. The whole `-c` call exits non-zero before its third
      print line ever runs. observeFingerprint never checks this call's
      exit status/error before parsing its stdout, so the resulting
      "observed" values are wrong in a way the fingerprint comparison then
      (correctly, given wrong inputs) reports as a mismatch.
Neither is fixed in src/ (this plan edits no src/ file — see the plan's own
kept scope). The throwaway probe script's injected execFn substitutes, ONLY
for this one "-c" version-probe call, `importlib.metadata.version()` (the
same mechanism Plan 21-02's own capture_fingerprint.py already uses, and
which RESEARCH.md verified live) — this changes HOW the version facts are
queried, never WHAT the environment's real facts are: independently
confirmed this session via `sha256sum` that scoreOneSha256 and both
cacheKeyFileSha256 entries (skb: and hub:) match the committed
fingerprint-manifest.json byte-for-byte, and the corrected version-probe
reproduces the committed manifest's pythonVersion/torchVersion/
starkQaVersion exactly (3.11.15 / 2.13.0 / 1.1.0). The REAL score_one.py
invocation (the one being timed) is passed straight through to the
unmodified spawnSync in every run below — this workaround never touches it.
This bug BLOCKS Phase 22's runner from calling stock runScoringPreflight
against this real environment with the default execFn and should be fixed
before Phase 22 begins (see the SUMMARY's finding).

